  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 AM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nLight showers over the Bighorn Basin will continue to exit toward MT \nin the next couple of hours this morning. Light showers over \nSweetwater County will gradually dissipate during this time as\nwell. Areas of low clouds/fog have developed this morning in the\nwake of yesterday's precipitation. This is expected to lift out\nby 16Z.  \n\nConvection will not be as extensive today, being more scattered \nalong and north of a Afton to Midwest line. Showers and \nthunderstorms will develop over far western portions by 18Z, moving \ntoward the northeast and moving over the Bighorn Basin by 21Z and \nthe Wind River Basin by 22Z/23Z. Heavy rain and localized flash \nflooding will continue to be the main threat from these storms as \nPWAT values remain abnormally high, ranging between 0.6\" to 1.1\". \nConvection will begin to end for most areas by 01Z, with showers and \nthunderstorms exiting Johnson County by 03Z. Winds (outside of\nany outflow boundary) will be a bit stronger today, with gusts \nof 20 to 25 mph occurring from Kemmerer to Casper with the \nstronger gusts occurring over South Pass and portions of Natrona\nCounty. Highs will continue to be slightly below normal, \nranging from the middle 70s to lower 80s.\n\nPrecipitation chances will not be as extensive over the weekend, \nwith convection being isolated across the CWA both days. \nTemperatures will continue to be slightly below normal Saturday and \nrising a few degrees on Sunday. This trend will continue into \nMonday, as the flow aloft becomes more zonal once again. A trough \nwill begin to drop southward over the PACNW/Northern Rockies \nTuesday, with a cold front moving over areas east of the Divide \nWednesday. Showers and thunderstorms will be possible Monday through \nWednesday, with Monday being the warmest day. Dry conditions look to \nreturn by Thursday as an area of high pressure rebuilds over the \nDesert Southwest and a ridge axis extending over ID. This will\nturn the flow aloft more northwesterly and likely pull wildfire\nsmoke back into the region.",
